This lecture focuses on three crucial error metrics used in regression analysis: Mean Squared Error (MSE), Mean Absolute Error (MAE), and Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE).

MSE: It calculates the average of the squared differences between actual and predicted values. It is preferred because it has a single global minimum, is differentiable, and converges quickly during optimization. However, MSE is sensitive to outliers, which can skew results.

MAE: This metric computes the average of the absolute differences between actual and predicted values. Its advantages include robustness to outliers and an interpretation that aligns with the original data's units. Nonetheless, MAE is less differentiable at zero, possibly leading to slower optimization.

RMSE: As the square root of MSE, RMSE shares benefits with MSE, such as being in original data units and being differentiable. However, similar to MSE, it is also affected by outliers.

The lecture concludes by highlighting the necessity of considering all three metrics for model evaluation and encourages practitioners to understand their pros and cons for discussions in interviews.

